# TopAI Deterministic Engineering Guidelines (.cursorrules)

These rules guide the Cursor AI coding assistant to produce high-performance, deterministic, and defensively engineered code across TypeScript, Python, and Go.

## 1. Principles of Deterministic Execution
- Always favor pure, predictable functions with explicit inputs and outputs over hidden state mutations.
- Prefer bounded execution and fail-fast invariants (validate inputs at boundary with sub-1ms assertions).
- Avoid implicit type coercions, undocumented fallbacks, and silent error swallows.
- Always use RFC-7807 structured Problem Details for API error responses.

## 2. Defensive Coding & Type Safety
- TypeScript: Enforce strict mode, avoid any, use discriminated unions for state modeling, and validate external inputs with runtime schemas (Zod/Valibot/TypeBox).
- Python: Use strict type hints, Pydantic models for data validation, and avoid bare except blocks.
- Concurrency: Prevent race conditions with explicit timeouts and idempotency keys on mutating operations.

## 3. FinOps & Context Efficiency
- Structure code cleanly to avoid deep nesting and verbose boilerplate.
- Keep function signatures minimal and self-describing to save LLM context window during maintenance.
- Prefer efficient built-in algorithms over bloated third-party dependencies.

## 4. Security & Data Sovereignty
- Zero-Retention: Never print, log, or persist sensitive credentials, API keys, or raw PII in code or logs.
- SSRF Defense: When designing URL fetchers or proxies, strictly disallow RFC-1918 private IPs, loopback, link-local, and resolve DNS before connecting.
- All network operations must have explicit connection and read timeouts.